  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mohamadali Abdulaziz Alwadkar vs State Of Maharashtra on 21 September, 2023 Author: M. S. Karnik Bench: M. S. Karnik 2023:Bhc-As:27939 Pmb 11.ba.2543-23.odt In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Bail Application No.2543 Of 2023 Mohamadali Abdulaziz Alwadkar ..Applicant Vs. The State Of Maharashtra ..Respondent ------------ Mr. Aniket Ujjwal Nikam a/w Mr. Aashish Satpute i/b. Ms. Veera Shinde, App for the State. ------------ Coram : M. S. Karnik, J. Date : September 21, 2023 P.C. : 1.",
    "facts": "This is an application for bail in respect of the offence punishable under Sections 328, 188, 272, 273 of the Indian Penal Code (hereafter 'IPC' for short) and under Section 59 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006 registered on 27.07.2023 vide C.R. No.471 of 2023 with Islampur Police Station, Sangli. The date of the incident is 27.07.2023. While on patrolling duty the complainant found a truck stationed near PMB 11.ba.2543-23.odt the national highway. The driver of the truck (present applicant) ran away. On inspection, the complainant found Gutka/Tobacco and scented pan masala in the said truck. The applicant was arrested on 31.07.2023. It is the contention of learned counsel for the applicant that the materials did not belong to the applicant. All offences except Section 328 of the IPC are bailable in nature. Section 59 of the Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006 is non- bailable. So far as Section 328 is concerned, the issue whether the substance seized will come within the ambit of Section 328 of the IPC is pending before the Hon'ble Supreme Court. The entire goods have been seized. There are no criminal antecedents reported against the applicant. Though the charge-sheet is yet to be filed, the custody of the applicant is not required.",
